​Here is exactly how you prototype this, step-by-step. Focus only on the "Happy Path"—the journey of one single mangrove forest turning into one verifiable digital token.



​Step 1: The "Brain" (Simulated MRV)
​Do not try to build a complex AI that parses live satellite data. You will run out of time.
Instead, fire up your code editor and spin up a simple local Python server using Flask or FastAPI. Write a script that takes an uploaded aerial photo, uses a basic library like OpenCV to calculate the percentage of "green pixels," and spits out a simple "Biomass Score." As long as an image goes in and a mathematically consistent number comes out, your "AI" is good enough to show the judges the logic.



​Step 2: The "Ledger" (Blockchain)
​Do not get tangled in complex, enterprise-grade blockchain deployments.
Write a single, simple Smart Contract using Solidity. Run a local test network right on your machine (using tools like Hardhat or Ganache). Your contract only needs to do one thing: it needs a function that takes that Biomass Score and mints a digital token to an account address. That is your carbon credit.



​Step 3: The "Bridge" (Integration)
​Your Python server needs to talk to your local blockchain. You can use a library like web3.py to do this easily. The logic is a straight line:
​The server receives the image.
​The server calculates the score.
​The server immediately triggers the smart contract to issue the token on the blockchain.



​Step 4: The "Face" (The Dashboard)
​Judges need something clean to look at. Build a simple web page. It only needs three things:
​A button to upload the "satellite" image.
​A nice loading spinner that says “Analyzing Biomass & Verifying...”
​A success screen that proudly displays “Carbon Credit Issued!” along with the actual transaction hash generated by your local blockchain.